  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+5})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ \u27e8\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,3(t-2)(t+4)=0\\,\u21d2\\,t=2,-4, \\\\ \\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,3(t-2)(t+12)=0\\,\u21d2\\,t=2,-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary.",
